Kate: Quick question — we've got a microservice that streams token chunks from a downstream LLM endpoint using HttpClient, and intermittently the consumer hangs forever when Polly retries are enabled. We create/dispose HttpClient per request right now. Anyone seen this pattern?

Raj: Creating a new HttpClient per request is the common smell. Can you paste the minimal repro (how you call GetAsync + Polly wrapper + stream read)?

Kate: Sure, simplified: 
```csharp
using var client = new HttpClient();
var policy = Policy.Handle<Exception>().WaitAndRetryAsync(3, i => TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(200));
await policy.ExecuteAsync(async () => {
  var resp = await client.GetAsync(url, HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead);
  resp.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
  using var stream = await resp.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
  using var sr = new StreamReader(stream);
  while (!sr.EndOfStream) {
    var line = await sr.ReadLineAsync();
    // process chunk
  }
});
```
This sometimes leaves the caller stuck on ReadLineAsync and subsequent requests exhaust sockets.

Tom: Yep — two issues: 1) disposing HttpClient per request defeats connection pooling -> socket exhaustion. 2) Polly retries the whole delegate, which can leave previous response/connection not properly drained/closed if the code doesn't fully complete. When streaming you want to avoid retrying the streaming read itself.

Irene: Agreed. Prefer using IHttpClientFactory (or a singleton HttpClient) so connections are pooled. Also configure SocketsHttpHandler settings (PooledConnectionLifetime / MaxConnectionsPerServer) depending on workload. Another practical tip: use HttpCompletionOption.ResponseHeadersRead (you already do) but make sure the retry policy only surrounds the initial GetAsync/headers stage, not the streaming loop.

Tom: Example pattern we've used: do the request under retry until you get a 2xx and the headers, then start reading outside the retry. Pseudo: 
```csharp
var resp = await retryPolicy.ExecuteAsync(() => client.GetAsync(url, ResponseHeadersRead));
resp.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
await using var stream = await resp.Content.ReadAsStreamAsync();
// read stream without retrying the whole call
```
Also set request-level cancellation tokens and timeouts; if a retry is triggered you must cancel/Dispose the previous response stream to free the socket.

Kate: OK that makes sense. Is there a Polly config that helps (so we don't wrap the read)? I had a single ExecuteAsync that included the read loop.

Irene: Split responsibilities: one policy for the request handshake, a different (lighter) policy for transient header failures. Eg: 
```csharp
var headerPolicy = Policy.Handle<HttpRequestException>()
  .WaitAndRetryAsync(2, _ => TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(150));
var resp = await headerPolicy.ExecuteAsync(() => client.GetAsync(url, ResponseHeadersRead));
```
Then read the stream outside any retry. Avoid Handle<Exception>() which catches everything (including TaskCanceledException). Also exclude TaskCanceledException/IOException from retry conditions so you don't retry reads that hang.

Tom: If you must support retry across the whole operation, ensure you fully consume or Dispose the existing response before retrying, and consider increasing MaxConnectionsPerServer as a stopgap. But the clean solution is split retry from stream.

Kate: One more thing — we observed TaskCanceledException when the server closes idle connection and the client tries to reuse it. Would SocketsHttpHandler.PooledConnectionLifetime short circuit that?

Tom: Yes, set PooledConnectionLifetime to slightly less than the server's keepalive so you don't get reused dead connections. Also set PooledConnectionIdleTimeout. Example: 
```csharp
new SocketsHttpHandler { PooledConnectionLifetime = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2), MaxConnectionsPerServer = 100 }
```

Kate: Great. I'll refactor to IHttpClientFactory + split the retry around GetAsync headers, add cancellation tokens, and tighten handler lifetimes. Thanks :pray:

